window.docContents[92].resolve({"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/Definition-6___5___1___-Dimension-vector/#Chapter6___Definition6___5___1":{"contents":"Definition 6.5.1. Let Q be a quiver with any labeling 1, \\ldots, n of the vertices. Let V = (V_1, \\ldots, V_n) be a representation of Q. We then call\n\nd(V) = (\\dim V_1, \\ldots, \\dim V_n)\n\nthe dimension vector of this representation.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.5. Gabriel's theorem\tDefinition 6.5.1: Dimension vector","header":"Definition 6.5.1: Dimension vector","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/Definition-6___5___1___-Dimension-vector/#Chapter6___Definition6___5___1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Theorem2___1___2":{"contents":"As a final example consider the representation theory of finite groups, which is one of the most\nfascinating chapters of representation theory. In this theory, one considers representations of\nthe group algebra A = \\mathbb{C}[G] of a finite group G — the algebra with basis a_g,\ng \\in G, and multiplication law a_g a_h = a_{gh}. We will show that any finite dimensional\nrepresentation of A is a direct sum of irreducible representations, i.e., the notions of an\nirreducible and indecomposable representation are the same for A (Maschke's theorem). Another\nstriking result discussed below is the Frobenius divisibility theorem: the dimension of any\nirreducible representation of A divides the order of G. Finally, we will show how to use the\nrepresentation theory of finite groups to prove Burnside's theorem: any finite group of order\np^a q^b, where p, q are primes, is solvable. Note that this theorem does not mention\nrepresentations, which are used only in its proof; a purely group-theoretical proof of this\ntheorem (not using representations) exists but is much more difficult!\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.1. What is representation theory?\tDynkin diagrams significance; representation theory of finite groups overview","header":"Dynkin diagrams significance; representation theory of finite groups overview","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Dynkin-diagrams-significance_SEMI_-representation-theory-of-finite-groups-overview/#Chapter2___Discussion_after_Theorem2___1___2"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/#chapter-03":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory","header":"3. General results of representation theory","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/#chapter-03"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___14___heading-1":{"contents":"Denote by U_\\lambda the representation \\operatorname{Ind}_{P_\\lambda}^{S_n} \\mathbb{C}. It is easy to see that U_\\lambda can be alternatively defined as U_\\lambda = \\mathbb{C}[S_n] a_\\lambda.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.14. Induced representations for $S_n$\tSection 5.14: Induced representations for S_n and definition of U_lambda","header":"5.14. Induced representations for  S_n","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___14___heading-1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Section-7___2___-Functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___2___heading-1":{"contents":"We would like to define arrows between categories. Such arrows are called functors.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.2. Functors\tSection 7.2: Functors","header":"7.2. Functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Section-7___2___-Functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___2___heading-1"}});
